  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1153 AM MST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nEast Idaho remains under the influence of an upper-level ridge \nof high pressure, but upstream satellite imagery shows a deep \nNorth Pacific closed low with multiple shortwave features \nrotating around its base toward the PacNW. The first feature \nmoving through Oregon today will bring mid- and high-level cloud\ncover to the region. A sprinkle or stray flurry is possible \ntonight across higher elevations to the north, but no measurable\nprecipitation is expected. The second feature moves through \nMonday night with slightly better moisture content. Isolated to \nscattered light showers are possible north of a line from \nKetchum to Driggs, mainly concentrated over the higher terrain. \n90th percentile QPF amounts max out between 0.10 and 0.15 inches\nacross the Sawtooths, with ensemble means running less than \n0.05 inches. The main impact will be a minor reduction in \ndaytime temperatures heading into Tuesday, dropping about 5 \ndegrees.\n\nMoving into the latter half of the week, the upper ridge\nrebuilds ahead of a developing split-flow system off the West\nCoast. Deterministic runs continue to trend toward digging the\nsouthern branch well south, leaving eastern Idaho under\nstrengthened dry southerly flow. Ensemble cluster uncertainty\nremains regarding how far inland the northern stream trough can\npush by Friday. Consequently, ensemble means and the NBM still\nintroduce light precipitation to the central mountains as early\nas Friday night, spreading across the remainder of East Idaho by\nSunday.",
